1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,370
VoltRelay grew fast:

2
00:00:01,370 --> 00:00:04,350
completed swaps nearly tripled in eighteen months,

3
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:08,050
and monthly revenue went from six to almost twenty million rupees.

4
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:11,250
But failed swaps spiked every summer,

5
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:13,240
new riders stopped coming back,

6
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:16,690
and each swap now loses more money than it did after last

7
00:00:16,690 --> 00:00:17,910
year's price rise.

8
00:00:18,730 --> 00:00:21,660
Leadership has four budget proposals on the table.

9
00:00:22,090 --> 00:00:24,960
Our job was to find out what is actually driving these

10
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:27,430
outcomes, before any money is spent.

11
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:31,610
We analysed all 3.9 million swap attempts,

12
00:00:31,610 --> 00:00:34,070
hourly station telemetry, battery records,

13
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:36,130
rider profiles and support tickets,

14
00:00:36,130 --> 00:00:37,750
in DuckDB and Python.

15
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:39,660
First we cleaned:

16
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:42,800
we removed two test stations that had billed almost four

17
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:47,140
million rupees, fixed a firmware bug that shifted 139,000

18
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:49,460
timestamps by five and a half hours,

19
00:00:49,470 --> 00:00:52,440
and re-read ticket comments in English and Hinglish.

20
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:54,850
Then we followed one chain:

21
00:00:54,850 --> 00:00:56,990
equipment, to service, to retention,

22
00:00:56,990 --> 00:00:58,140
to unit economics.

23
00:00:58,940 --> 00:00:59,840
Insight one:

24
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:01,830
failures are not spread evenly.

25
00:01:02,290 --> 00:01:05,230
They concentrate in Jaipur, Delhi and Hyderabad,

26
00:01:05,230 --> 00:01:06,000
in summer.

27
00:01:06,830 --> 00:01:08,300
The telemetry shows why:

28
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:12,820
above 45 degrees, a first-generation cabinet needs 156

29
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:15,530
minutes to charge a pack instead of 88,

30
00:01:15,530 --> 00:01:19,300
and runs out of charged batteries in 37 percent of hours.

31
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:25,020
Just 36 Gen1 stations in those three cities cause 57.5

32
00:01:25,020 --> 00:01:27,000
percent of all summer failures.

33
00:01:27,790 --> 00:01:28,770
Insight two:

34
00:01:28,770 --> 00:01:31,110
three bad battery lots from one supplier.

35
00:01:31,570 --> 00:01:34,050
They lost health 2.4 times faster,

36
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:37,970
giving riders 49 kilometres per swap instead of 61,

37
00:01:37,970 --> 00:01:41,830
and costing 82 rupees of wear per swap instead of 35.

38
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:46,430
Those lots explain three quarters of this year's margin loss.

39
00:01:46,870 --> 00:01:47,970
Riders noticed:

40
00:01:47,970 --> 00:01:51,660
nine thousand tickets filed as 'other' were really battery complaints.

41
00:01:52,470 --> 00:01:53,550
Insight three:

42
00:01:53,570 --> 00:01:56,940
new riders who hit two or more failed swaps in their first

43
00:01:56,940 --> 00:01:59,880
two weeks churn at 15 percent instead of 9.

44
00:02:00,310 --> 00:02:03,030
Price plan, channel and partner don't matter.

45
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:05,410
And the peak-pricing pilot?

46
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:09,410
Plus eight rupees a swap, but almost no demand shifted.

47
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:12,390
It's a revenue lever, not a congestion fix.

48
00:02:12,850 --> 00:02:16,310
Meanwhile the biggest partner, on a 28 percent discount,

49
00:02:16,310 --> 00:02:17,670
is the least profitable.

50
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:20,400
So, our recommendations.

51
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:21,310
One:

52
00:02:21,310 --> 00:02:24,960
replace the 36 Gen1 cabinets in the three hot cities

53
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:29,630
before next summer, which avoids about 22,600 failures a year.

54
00:02:30,090 --> 00:02:30,400
Two:

55
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,000
pull the bad-lot packs that are still in service,

56
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,320
replace them, and buy on lot testing,

57
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:36,600
not supplier name.

58
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:37,990
Three:

59
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:40,450
protect every new rider's first fortnight,

60
00:02:40,490 --> 00:02:43,890
with healthy packs and an automatic credit after any failure.

61
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:44,730
Four:

62
00:02:44,730 --> 00:02:48,060
renegotiate ZipDrop instead of signing an exclusive.

63
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:50,940
Fix the equipment and the batteries,

64
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:52,680
and growth becomes profitable.

65
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:53,740
Thank you.

